# Sub-projects that ship independently — exclude from Vercel build context.
# Each has its own deploy pipeline (or none yet).
helm-website-ui/
helm-intelligence/
next-shadcn-dashboard-starter/
archive/
.full-review/
.full-review-archive-*/
.full-stack-feature-archived-*/

# Local-only artifacts
docs/superpowers/
*.pptx

# Build + test output.
#
# These are already in .gitignore, which would normally be enough — but the
# MOMENT this file exists, Vercel uses it INSTEAD of .gitignore rather than in
# addition to it. So everything .gitignore was quietly keeping out of the
# upload came back the first time anyone ran `vercel --prod` from a working
# directory that had been built or tested in.
#
# That is not theoretical: a deploy on 2026-08-03 was rejected outright —
#   "`files` should NOT have more than 15000 items, received 48139"
# — because .next alone was 13 GB of Turbopack cache at the time.
.next/
node_modules/
test-results/
playwright-report/
coverage/
.turbo/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Local-only tooling state — same .gitignore-is-ignored trap as the block
# above, and the reason the 2026-08-09 deploy was rejected at 19,795 files:
#
#   .deepsec    26,425 files — per-repo security-scanner working directory
#   .worktrees   7,004 files — git worktrees, i.e. a FULL SOURCE COPY of every
#                              in-flight branch, uploaded alongside the branch
#                              actually being deployed
#
# Both are in .gitignore and neither has ever been part of a build. Listing
# them here is what makes .gitignore's intent apply to the upload too.
.deepsec/
.worktrees/
